<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mercantilism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mercantilism was an economic doctrine dominant in early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, holding that national wealth depended on accumulating bullion and maintaining a favourable balance of trade. It encouraged state-sponsored trading companies, colonies and tariffs. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> it was associated above all with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a>, minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mercantilism.jpg" alt="Mercantilism" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claude Lorrain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F0087_Louvre_Gellee_port_au_soleil_couchant-_INV4715_rwk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Mercantilism became the dominant school of economic thought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> and the early modern period, before the rise of classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>. Mercantilist practices appeared early in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, in their control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> trade in bullion. The Italian economist Antonio Serra wrote one of the first treatises on political economy in 1613. The term was first used only by critics such as Mirabeau and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a>, and the older English term was the mercantile system. Smith saw the English merchant Thomas Mun as a major creator of the system through Treasure by Foreign Trade, published after his death in 1664.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In its simplest form mercantilism is bullionism, the view that a nation's wealth is measured by the gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> it holds. Its writers were concerned with the movement of money more than with hoarding. The aim was to maximise exports and minimise imports and to build state power at the expense of rivals. Policies included high tariffs, banning the export of gold and silver, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navigation-acts">Navigation Acts</a>, which forbade trade to be carried in foreign ships.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> it is closely tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a>, finance minister for 22 years in the 17th century, so that French mercantilism is sometimes called Colbertism. Under him industries were organised into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> and monopolies, foreign artisans were brought in, and roads and canals were built. French industrial output grew considerably, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> stayed supreme in trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Mercantilism fed imperial expansion, as European states conquered colonies as sources of gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and as exclusive markets, often through chartered monopolies such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>. If trade is seen as fixed, one state can increase its trade only by taking it from another, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-wars">Anglo-Dutch Wars</a> can be linked to such theories.</p><p>In the 18th century critics undermined it. In 1690 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a> argued that prices vary in proportion to the quantity of money, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-hume">David Hume</a> showed that a constant surplus of trade cannot last. Smith criticised the doctrine as a system serving traders, though he praised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navigation-acts">Navigation Acts</a> for their effect on British shipping.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mercantilism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The economy of the first empire rested on <b>mercantilism</b> and plantation slavery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Capitalism in its modern form is traced to agrarian capitalism and <b>mercantilism</b> in the early Renaissance, in city-states like Florence.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Its “First” empire, based on <b>mercantilism</b> in North America, the Caribbean and India, was checked by the loss of the American colonies in 1776; it turned to India, Australia, Asia, Africa and the Pacific, and after 1815 enjoyed a century of almost unchallenged dominance, the Pax Britannica, which…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>The historian Armitage sees the song as the most lasting expression of the idea of Britain and the British Empire that emerged in the 1730s, built on &quot;a mixture of adulterated <b>mercantilism</b>, nationalistic anxiety and libertarian fervour&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rule-britannia">Rule, Britannia!</a></li></ul></section>
